        ":question/id": "104caed5-f979-595f-b6af-9b7ac36edd14",
        ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{- 6 x - 2}{- 4 x^{2} - 9 x - 4}$, find $f^{\\prime}(x).$\n\n$f^{\\prime}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The numerator and denominator are $u(x)=- 6 x - 2$ and $v(x)=- 4 x^{2} - 9 x - 4,$ respectively. Their derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u^{\\prime}(x)&=-6,\\\\v^{\\prime}(x)&=- 8 x - 9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{\\prime}(x)&=\\frac{u^{\\prime}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot v^{\\prime}(x)}{[v(x)]^2}\\\\ &=\\frac{(-6)\\cdot (- 4 x^{2} - 9 x - 4)-(- 6 x - 2)\\cdot (- 8 x - 9)}{(- 4 x^{2} - 9 x - 4)^2}\\\\ &=\\frac{- 24 x^{2} - 16 x + 6}{\\left(- 4 x^{2} - 9 x - 4\\right)^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{- 24 x^{2} - 16 x + 6}{\\left(- 4 x^{2} - 9 x - 4\\right)^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{- 24 x^{2} - 16 x + 6}{\\left(- 4 x^{2} - 9 x - 4\\right)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
